> complex log(-1)
log(-1) = i*pi
  Re = 0,  Im = pi
  check: equal numerically (3.141592654i, complex arithmetic)
> complex sqrt(-1)
sqrt(-1) = i
  Re = 0,  Im = 1
  check: equal numerically (1i, complex arithmetic)
> complex (-1)^(1/2)
(-1)^(1/2) = (-1)^(1/2)
  Re = (-1)^(1/2),  Im = 0
  check: equal numerically (1i, complex arithmetic)
> complex exp(i*pi)
exp(i*pi) = -1
  Re = -1,  Im = 0
  check: equal numerically (-1, complex arithmetic)
> complex i^i
i^i = exp(-pi/2)
  Re = exp(-pi/2),  Im = 0
  check: equal numerically (0.2078795764, complex arithmetic)
> polar 1 + i
1 + i = sqrt(2)*e^(i*pi/4)
  = sqrt(2)*(cos(pi/4) + i*sin(pi/4))
  r = |z| = sqrt(2),  theta = arg z = pi/4
  theta is the principal argument, -pi < theta <= pi
  check: equal numerically (1 + 1i, complex arithmetic)
  check: equal numerically (1 + 1i, complex arithmetic)
> re log(z)
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
  note: z = x + iy (real and imaginary parts real)
  note: i is the imaginary unit; x, y are taken as real
Re(log(x + i*y)) = log(x^2 + y^2)/2
  check: equal at 64 of 64 sample points (max relative difference 2e-16)
> im sqrt(z)
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
  note: z = x + iy (real and imaginary parts real)
  note: i is the imaginary unit; x, y are taken as real
Im(sqrt(x + i*y)) = copysign(sqrt((sqrt(x^2 + y^2) - x)/2), y)
  check: equal at 64 of 64 sample points (max relative difference 1e-15)
> arg -1
arg(-1) = pi
  the principal argument, -pi < arg <= pi
  check: equal numerically (3.141592654, complex arithmetic)
> nthroots 3 1
the 3 cube roots of 1 (w^3 = 1)
  w0 = 1                      = 1  (principal)
  w1 = -1/2 + i*sqrt(3)/2     = e^(i*2pi/3)
  w2 = -1/2 - i*sqrt(3)/2     = e^(-i*2pi/3)
  check: each w^3, parsed back as printed, equals 1 numerically
> holomorphic 1/z
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
  holomorphic: the Cauchy-Riemann equations hold at 64 sample points of [-2, 2] x [-2, 2] (df/dx = df/(i dy))
  holomorphic except at the points where it is undefined (poles)
> holomorphic conj(z)
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
conj(z) is not holomorphic
  Cauchy-Riemann fails: at z = 1/2 + i/4, df/dx = 1 but df/(i dy) = -1
  (it uses conj, which is not holomorphic)
  Wirtinger derivatives there: df/dz = 0, df/dconj(z) = 1 (holomorphic means df/dconj(z) = 0)
> residue 1/(z^4+1) at exp(i*pi/4)
  note: z is taken as complex (no x or y here; say z: real for a real variable)
residue of 1/(z^4 + 1) at z = sqrt(2)/2 + i*sqrt(2)/2:
  = -sqrt(2)/8 - i*sqrt(2)/8
  a pole of order 1 (|f| grows like |z - a|^-1)
  computed as (1/(2 pi i)) times the integral of 1/(z^4 + 1) on |z - a| = 0.01 and 0.005 (512 points each): they agree
